  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mobilizing grandmothers, community and religious leaders to promote girls&apos; rights and education</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project operates in the Kolda region, an area where girls&apos; education is considered less important than that of boys; and where early marriage, teenage pregnancy and female genital mutilation (FGM) are also major constraints for girls. The project aims then to: -Change the attitudes and practices of the generations of men and women, notables and religious leaders on human rights (HR) in relation to girls&apos; education, early marriage, early pregnancy and FGM. -Strengthen the actions taken by the generations of women and men to promote the rights and development of young girls; -Disseminate the Change through Culture approach to promote HR to other NGOs and institutions in Senegal. It intends to implement community activities for a holistic consideration of the concerns of girls, to promote the rights and welfare of girls. These include the organization of Days of Dialogue and Solidarity (DDS), several &#x201c;sessions under the tree&#x201d;, women and youth forums , which are important activities for the promotion of human rights. In addition, GMP has developed a model approach called Change through Culture (CBC) which it aims to disseminate during this project to other local organizations working on human rights issues. Several training workshops will be held for these organizations in the regions of Kolda, S&#xe9;dhiou, Ziguinchor, Tambacounda and K&#xe9;dougou. Other issues that have a negative impact on girls are the lack of communication between generations and between men and women, and the traditional directive style of communication from older to younger people and from men to women. To address these issues, the HR programme also seeks to promote: changes in gender roles and attitudes to make them more equitable at the family level and in community life; strengthening communication between the three generations (elders, adults, adolescents, men and women) and between the sexes. Other activities included in the project are: monitoring and ongoing documentation of activities; production of two videos on the two key activities (above); a results document; and meetings to present the project results at departmental and national level.</narrative>
  </description>
